---
/**
 * Workflow — how metis is actually driven: two terminals, a coder agent and a
 * reviewer agent, trading four short prompts while the protocol carries the
 * context between them.
 *
 * Copy is rendered verbatim from docs/copy.md §Workflow (ADR-0003: the copy
 * deck is the single source of truth). Every copy string sits on ONE source
 * line and is never split by an inline element, so the built output greps
 * literally against the deck (convention from phase-1-ws-1.1, documented in
 * 1.2 and 1.3).
 *
 * --- Three strings per step, not one ------------------------------------
 *
 * The deck publishes each step on a single markdown line carrying three
 * distinct things: an attribution ("Terminal 1 — the coder:"), a quoted
 * prompt, and a parenthetical caption. They render here as three sibling
 * elements, each holding one uninterrupted text node, split only at the
 * deck's own bold/quote/italic boundaries — never inside a string.
 *
 * A single text node was not available: the brief requires the prompt to read
 * as human words and the caption to read as a caption, which needs two
 * elements with different typography. The deck already treats separately
 * rendered strings as separate fields elsewhere (§Personas publishes a column
 * heading and its body as two fields; §Proof publishes each stat's value and
 * label as two fields), so this is the deck's own convention applied to a line
 * it happened to write compactly.
 *
 * The quote marks around each prompt are rendered as the real characters the
 * deck writes. They ARE the quoted treatment the brief asks for, and copy
 * characters on this site are never generated from CSS (the rule
 * Problem.astro records for its em dashes).
 *
 * The backticked commands inside the captions and the closing line
 * (`metis kickoff`, `metis next`, `coded`, `reviewed`) render as plain text
 * rather than <code>: the same call Protocol.astro:27-30 made for
 * `metis next`, Install.astro:18-22 for the `go install` line and
 * Hero.astro:76-83 for `AGENTS.md`. A <code> element inside a sentence splits
 * it into three text nodes and breaks the literal grep, and that is not a
 * trade worth making for typography alone.
 *
 * --- Nothing here is a shell command ------------------------------------
 *
 * The four prompts are words a human types AT an agent. There is no `$` glyph
 * anywhere in this section, no command line and no output block: the plan's
 * "prompt glyph" is honoured as terminal chrome — a decorative caret bar in
 * each card's title bar, beside the attribution — and never in front of the
 * human words, which is exactly what would make a prompt read as a command.
 * The captions are prose in parentheses, in the mono face because they narrate
 * what the tool did; they are captions, not transcripts.
 *
 * Every CLI claim in the captions and the closing line was re-verified against
 * metis 0.0.6 in the session that wrote this component, rather than inherited
 * from the deck's verification note: `metis kickoff` prints the session
 * protocol; `metis next -o json` returns ONE active-slice object, never a list
 * (deterministic dispatch, "gets dispatched one slice"); `metis commit --help`
 * lists `--brief`, `--flip coded|reviewed` and `--agent`, the last described
 * as "required with --flip reviewed for cross-vendor validation" ("the final
 * flip demands a different agent's identity"); scope and checklist auditing
 * are `metis log <id> --validate`, `metis block` and `metis findings record`
 * ("audits scope and the checklist, then files findings"); and kickoff step 5
 * documents `metis verify`'s distinct exit codes 0/1/2 ("exit codes do the
 * arguing").
 *
 * One deck claim is NOT verifiable from this page and is recorded as an
 * advisory finding rather than silently rendered as true or quietly reworded:
 * the closing line says the prompts "stay four words long", while the four
 * prompts the deck publishes run five to seven words. docs/copy.md is
 * read-only in this slice's scope and ADR-0003 makes the deck the only place
 * that string may be changed, so it renders verbatim here and the fix belongs
 * to the deck's owner.
 *
 * --- The staircase -------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * <ol> carries the order, so the exchange reads correctly with CSS disabled
 * and the layout can be purely presentational. It carries an explicit
 * role="list" because `list-style: none` makes WebKit drop the list role, and
 * the order that role conveys is what the layout is illustrating (f-005).
 *
 * From 768px the odd steps take the left column and the even steps the right,
 * one step per row — a staircase. The alternation is the point: reading down
 * the page ping-pongs between the two terminals, which is what a loop between
 * two agents looks like. A 2×2 grid was rejected because it reads as two rows
 * of a pair rather than a hand-off, and connectors were rejected because
 * Protocol.astro already owns the connector idiom and the brief asks for a
 * simple alternating layout, not a second diagram. Below 768px it is one
 * column in document order, so the narrow layout needs no media query at all.
 *
 * 768px is a raw length on purpose, the trade Personas.astro:51-53 records:
 * tokens.css owns colour, type and spacing and deliberately defines no
 * breakpoint scale, so a layout threshold has to be written literally.
 *
 * --- Colour and contrast -------------------------------------------------
 *
 * Every pairing is already in tokens.css's ratio table; this section
 * introduces no new one. Muted on surface 7.66:1 dark / 6.06:1 light (the
 * attribution and the caption), text on surface 16.14:1 / 18.91:1 (the
 * prompt), muted on base 8.39:1 / 5.66:1 (the lead and the closing line) —
 * all AA. The card hairline is the decorative 1.40:1 --color-border used
 * throughout, sharpening an edge the surface fill already draws.
 *
 * The caret bar is decorative and carries no information: owl-gold on the
 * coder's cards, aegean on the reviewer's, purely so the two terminals are
 * separable at a glance. The attribution text names the terminal, so the
 * colour is redundant and nothing is lost when the pseudo-element is
 * unsupported, unstyled or unseen. Aegean is tokens.css's diagram accent and
 * this staircase is a diagram of the loop; gold stays sparing at two small
 * bars (OVERVIEW §3.2).
 *
 * --- Motion --------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * Reveal (ADR-0005): the section opts into the page's shared observer with
 * `data-reveal`, and its six blocks carry `.reveal` so they arrive in reading
 * order — heading, lead, the four turns in sequence, then the closing line.
 * This component declares no animation of its own, only `--reveal-delay`
 * custom properties that index.astro's rule reads; they are inert until
 * `is-revealed` lands and only outside reduced motion. Nothing sets a hidden
 * base state, so with JS off, the observer unsupported, or reduced motion set,
 * the section renders complete and immediately (ADR-0005's never-invisible
 * rule). No script is added — the page still carries exactly one.
 */
---

<section id="workflow" class="section" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="reveal">Run it from two terminals.</h2>

  <p class="lead reveal">One terminal codes. One terminal reviews. Neither sees the other's context — the ledger is the only shared state. Point them at different vendors — the pairing is config, not code — and let the protocol referee.</p>

  <ol class="exchange" role="list">
    <li class="turn reveal">
      <p class="who">Terminal 1 — the coder:</p>
      <p class="prompt">"Go for dev of the next slice."</p>
      <p class="did">(the agent runs metis kickoff, gets dispatched one slice, commits a brief, codes inside its declared scope, flips coded)</p>
    </li>

    <li class="turn reveal">
      <p class="who">Terminal 2 — the reviewer:</p>
      <p class="prompt">"Go for review of the next slice."</p>
      <p class="did">(a different agent audits scope and the checklist, then files findings — or flips reviewed)</p>
    </li>

    <li class="turn reveal">
      <p class="who">Terminal 1 — the coder:</p>
      <p class="prompt">"Fix the findings from review."</p>
      <p class="did">(rework lands on the same slice, same scope, same paper trail)</p>
    </li>

    <li class="turn reveal">
      <p class="who">Terminal 2 — the reviewer:</p>
      <p class="prompt">"Go for re-review — focus on the findings."</p>
      <p class="did">(the loop repeats until clean; the final flip demands a different agent's identity)</p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p class="closing reveal">The prompts stay four words long because the protocol carries the context: metis kickoff prints the contract, metis next picks the slice, and exit codes do the arguing.</p>
</section>

<style>
  .section {
    padding-block: var(--space-8);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  /* Same treatment as Problem's lead: one step up in size, muted, so it reads
     as the frame for what follows rather than as a second heading. */
  .lead {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-4);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  /* --- The exchange ------------------------------------------------------
     Mobile-first: one column, in document order. Drops the UA list indent
     with the token zero rather than a literal 0 — the scale defines the zero
     step, so spacing comes off the scale even when it is nothing (f-003). */
  .exchange {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
    padding: var(--space-0);
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* A terminal pane: raised surface for the boundary, hairline to sharpen the
     edge, the same shape the hero's transcript and the persona columns use. */
  .turn {
    padding: var(--space-4);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
  }

  /* Title bar. Mono, because this is terminal chrome; the rule beneath it
     separates the machine's frame from the human's words. */
  .who {
    padding-block-end: var(--space-3);
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  /* The caret bar — decorative chrome echoing the hero's "█", never a prompt
     glyph in front of the human words. Sized off the spacing scale for its
     width and off the line box for its height, so it tracks the type. */
  .who::before {
    content: "";
    display: inline-block;
    width: var(--space-1);
    height: 0.9em;
    margin-inline-end: var(--space-2);
    vertical-align: -0.1em;
    background-color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  /* The reviewer's terminal, in aegean. Decorative only: the attribution text
     is what names the terminal. */
  .turn:nth-child(even) .who::before {
    background-color: var(--color-secondary);
  }

  /* The prompt is a human sentence, so it takes the display face, a size up
     and full-strength text — the one thing in the card that is not machine
     output. The quotes come from the copy, not from CSS. */
  .prompt {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
    font-weight: var(--weight-medium);
  }

  /* What the tool then did: mono and muted, the site's idiom for machine
     behaviour, one step down so it sits under the prompt rather than beside
     it in importance. */
  .did {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  .closing {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  /* --- The staircase ----------------------------------------------------
     Two lanes, one step per row: the odd steps hang on the left, the even
     ones on the right, so reading down the page ping-pongs between the two
     terminals. The rows have to be stated explicitly — with only the columns
     set, auto-placement would pack steps 1 and 2 into the same row and the
     alternation would collapse into a 2×2 grid. */
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .exchange {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
      column-gap: var(--space-5);
    }

    .turn:nth-child(odd) {
      grid-column: 1;
    }

    .turn:nth-child(even) {
      grid-column: 2;
    }

    .turn:nth-child(1) {
      grid-row: 1;
    }

    .turn:nth-child(2) {
      grid-row: 2;
    }

    .turn:nth-child(3) {
      grid-row: 3;
    }

    .turn:nth-child(4) {
      grid-row: 4;
    }
  }

  /* --- Reveal stagger ----------------------------------------------------
     Choreography only: index.astro owns the animation, this component owns
     the order. The delays are inert custom properties until that rule reads
     them, which happens only once `is-revealed` lands and only outside
     reduced motion — so no media query is needed here.

     80ms apart in reading order, the h2 taking the 0ms default. The four
     turns arrive in sequence rather than as a block, because the sequence IS
     the content: the hand-off is what the section is about. The closing line
     lands last, at 480ms, so the section settles as one gesture. */
  .lead {
    --reveal-delay: 80ms;
  }

  .turn:nth-child(1) {
    --reveal-delay: 160ms;
  }

  .turn:nth-child(2) {
    --reveal-delay: 240ms;
  }

  .turn:nth-child(3) {
    --reveal-delay: 320ms;
  }

  .turn:nth-child(4) {
    --reveal-delay: 400ms;
  }

  .closing {
    --reveal-delay: 480ms;
  }
</style>
